\begin_layout Subsection
Explainability Measure
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "gp-Complexity-measurement"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How explainable something is is best described by the effort a person needs
 to understand a formula.
 Effort, however, is a very arbitrary property, which is why explainability
 cannot be clearly determined.
 In GP, therefore, no further distinction is usually made between explainability
 and the complexity of a formula.
 The number of nodes poses some problems as a paradigm, even after weighting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Divide and Conquer with the if-operator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although the if-operator is the most complex function with 3 input parameters,
 it creates more structure than all other operators because its child trees
 are not combined but can be considered separately.
 Nevertheless, the subtrees must have a relation to each other in order
 to harmonize together.
 This combination creates encapsulated programs in a clear way that humans
 can understand more easily.
 It is always to be welcomed when these are contained in programs, but this
 should not be artificially forced, as they are not useful at every point.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The if statement is already penalized by its high branching factor, which
 should not be further supported by a higher weighting.
 
\end_layout

